## FABRIC-SDK OSI MAPPING

fabric-invoke lives at L4 (Interceptor) and L6 (MCP):

  L6 (MCP)        — fabric-invoke exposes fabric_invoke, fabric_route, fabric_status
                    as MCP tools. Claude Desktop calls these at L6 to trigger the
                    full multi-agent pipeline with a single tool call.

  L4 (Interceptor) — Path execution is L4's job. fabric-router (also L4) decides.
                     fabric-invoke executes. Together they are the full interceptor:
                     routing decision → dispatch → context chaining → aggregation.

  Relationship to fabric-router:
    fabric-router: the brain (decides where to go)
    fabric-invoke: the legs (gets it there)
    Neither works without the other.

  Relationship to AIANA (L5):
    AIANA is called by fabric-invoke at both ends of every invocation.
    Recall: does AIANA know anything relevant to this query?
    Record: AIANA should know what we just learned.
    fabric-invoke is AIANA's most frequent writer — every resolved invocation
    adds a memory. Over time this is what makes the local models smarter.

  Full request lifecycle (L1 → L7):
    L1: Query arrives over Tailscale mesh
    L2: Firewall screens for injection / PII
    L3: Gateway receives, looks up F-RIB prefix
    L4: fabric-invoke calls fabric-router → dispatches to specialists → chains context
    L5: AIANA recall pre-dispatch, record post-resolution
    L6: MCP tool returns aggregated result to Claude Desktop
    L7: GitHub Actions / worker executes if a PR/workflow was created

  The escalation ladder (per ADR-001):
    deterministic (confidence ≥ 0.95) → tool execution, no LLM needed
    local-llm (confidence ≥ floor)    → specialist Ollama model handles it
    claude (0.0.0.0/0)                → fabric-invoke escalates to Claude API
                                         with full aggregated context prepended

  As AIANA's feedback loop matures, escalation rate to Claude trends toward zero.
  fabric-invoke is the mechanism by which that loop closes.
"""
